KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ unnamed parameters in the definition: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [temp.param]/15, [temp.local]/7: `template <class C, class = C>
struct S;' followed by the definition `template <class, class> struct S'
gives S<char> the second argument char.  The default argument is
evaluated where the definition's parameters have no names: "symbol 'C' is
unknown", and main is not type-checked (bin53 too).  libstdc++ declares
`template<typename _CharT, typename _Traits = char_traits<_CharT>> class
basic_ostream;' in <iosfwd> and defines it with named parameters, so
this is not the <sstream> drop.  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this
program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
